{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0129HEGLsDFgJ7Y7NcNQHYqv","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t**Review of the job-status API (`api.py`, `store.py`)**\n2\t\n3\t**High: GET has no tenant check, so any tenant can read another tenant's results** (`repo/api.py:7-9`)\n4\t\n5\tThe GET branch reads `store.jobs[handle]` directly and returns `job.result`. It never compares `job.tenant` to `identity.tenant`. The README says handles can appear in shared support URLs and that possession grants no authority. So anyone with a leaked or shared handle can read another tenant's data. DELETE already does this correctly through `store.owned(...)`.\n6\t\n7\tFix:\n8\t```python\n9\tif request.method == 'GET':\n10\t    job = store.owned(identity.tenant, handle)\n11\t    return {'result': job.result}\n12\t```\n13\t\n14\t**Medium: handle existence leaks, and unknown handles cause unhandled errors** (`repo/store.py:16`, `repo/api.py:8`)\n15\t- `self.jobs[handle]` raises `KeyError` for an unknown handle. A handle that exists under another tenant raises `Forbidden`. A caller can tell \"exists but isn't mine\" from \"doesn't exist\", which confirms that a leaked or guessed handle is real.\n16\t- The `KeyError` is probably an unhandled 500, unless the host maps it.\n17\t- Make `owned` raise the same not-found error for both cases:\n18\t  ```python\n19\t  job = self.jobs.get(handle)\n20\t  if job is None or job.tenant != tenant:\n21\t      raise NotFound()\n22\t  ```\n23\t- The host would need to map `Forbidden` and `NotFound` to 403 and 404. That mapping is outside the code I can see.\n24\t\n25\t**Low / hardening**\n26\t- **`create` stores the request body as `result`** (`repo/store.py:11-13`, `repo/api.py:5`). The caller controls what is later returned as the job result. The worker is external, so this may be a placeholder. If not, results should come only from the worker, and the body should be validated or stored as job input. Body validation is said to happen at ingress, which I can't see.\n27\t- **No lifecycle management.** `Store.jobs` is an unbounded in-memory dict with no TTL, eviction or per-tenant quota. That risks memory exhaustion and keeps results for as long as the process runs. It is also not shared across workers or processes.\n28\t- **A cancelled job still returns its result on GET.** `cancelled` is set but never checked on read. Confirm that's intended.\n29\t- **Unused import:** `Forbidden` in `api.py:1`.\n30\t- **Unsupported methods raise a bare `ValueError`** (`repo/api.py:14`), which likely becomes a 500. Return a 405 instead. The same applies to POST on any path other than `/jobs`, which falls through to `path_params['job_id']` and can raise `KeyError`.\n31\t\n32\t**Good**\n33\t- Handles come from `secrets.token_urlsafe(24)`, which is unguessable.\n34\t- Tenant comes from the host-injected `verified_identity`, not from the request body.\n35\t- DELETE enforces ownership.\n36\t\n37\tI did not review `research/`. I read only the `repo/` files and ran nothing."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2b54c33e-469a-4d8c-85e5-5788bdd93c33","uuid":"c057c607-fd42-4d50-8938-fa432c2188db","timestamp":"2026-10-24T20:06:01.948Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-mcp-grader-vbbh2i3t/report.md","content":"**Review of the job-status API (`api.py`, `store.py`)**\n\n**High: GET has no tenant check, so any tenant can read another tenant's results** (`repo/api.py:7-9`)\n\nThe GET branch reads `store.jobs[handle]` directly and returns `job.result`. It never compares `job.tenant` to `identity.tenant`. The README says handles can appear in shared support URLs and that possession grants no authority. So anyone with a leaked or shared handle can read another tenant's data. DELETE already does this correctly through `store.owned(...)`.\n\nFix:\n```python\nif request.method == 'GET':\n    job = store.owned(identity.tenant, handle)\n    return {'result': job.result}\n```\n\n**Medium: handle existence leaks, and unknown handles cause unhandled errors** (`repo/store.py:16`, `repo/api.py:8`)\n- `self.jobs[handle]` raises `KeyError` for an unknown handle. A handle that exists under another tenant raises `Forbidden`. A caller can tell \"exists but isn't mine\" from \"doesn't exist\", which confirms that a leaked or guessed handle is real.\n- The `KeyError` is probably an unhandled 500, unless the host maps it.\n- Make `owned` raise the same not-found error for both cases:\n  ```python\n  job = self.jobs.get(handle)\n  if job is None or job.tenant != tenant:\n      raise NotFound()\n  ```\n- The host would need to map `Forbidden` and `NotFound` to 403 and 404. That mapping is outside the code I can see.\n\n**Low / hardening**\n- **`create` stores the request body as `result`** (`repo/store.py:11-13`, `repo/api.py:5`). The caller controls what is later returned as the job result. The worker is external, so this may be a placeholder. If not, results should come only from the worker, and the body should be validated or stored as job input. Body validation is said to happen at ingress, which I can't see.\n- **No lifecycle management.** `Store.jobs` is an unbounded in-memory dict with no TTL, eviction or per-tenant quota. That risks memory exhaustion and keeps results for as long as the process runs. It is also not shared across workers or processes.\n- **A cancelled job still returns its result on GET.** `cancelled` is set but never checked on read. Confirm that's intended.\n- **Unused import:** `Forbidden` in `api.py:1`.\n- **Unsupported methods raise a bare `ValueError`** (`repo/api.py:14`), which likely becomes a 500. Return a 405 instead. The same applies to POST on any path other than `/jobs`, which falls through to `path_params['job_id']` and can raise `KeyError`.\n\n**Good**\n- Handles come from `secrets.token_urlsafe(24)`, which is unguessable.\n- Tenant comes from the host-injected `verified_identity`, not from the request body.\n- DELETE enforces ownership.\n\nI did not review `research/`. I read only the `repo/` files and ran nothing.","numLines":37,"startLine":1,"totalLines":37}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_0129HEGLsDFgJ7Y7NcNQHYqv","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
